From caf-talk Caf Nov  1 17:03:18 1994
From: sears@uh.edu (Paul S. Sears)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Summary & Conclusion (was Re: Policy at UH (was Re: Academic syads who censor newsgroups ...))
Date: 1 Nov 1994 21:46:31 GMT
Message-ID: <396cvn$ast@masala.cc.uh.edu>

Summary and conclusion:

Well, we had a policy here at the Engineering computing center that  
basically said that viewing of sexually explicit images in the computer lab  
was prohibited.  After posting this, I was duely informed on how we  
can't/shouldn't do this, etc.

After, much meaningful discussion (with the obvious conclusion that I  
_really_ need to brush up on constitutional law :-), I stated that we would  
modify or even abolish the above stated policy if it could not stand up on  
its own legal feet.  Well, it seems that our policy tripped and fell, with a  
helping push from the University of Houston policies group.

We discussed our policy in a public and private forums.  The public forum  
stated that the policy is in violation of the First Amendment and since we  
are a public institution, we must honor academic freedom (true!)

The private forum (internal), initially agreed with our policy.  In fact,  
both the policies group and Affirmative Action stated that our policy is  
legally ok and that we should have such a policy plastered in every room in  
our facility...

Well, today, the nail was put in the coffin and after much debate that went  
from UH Legal to the President's office, it turns out that 

     "The bottom line is:  No, there are too many first amendment
     issues to attempt a policy prohibiting viewing such images."
     					- Judy Viebig
					UH Policies

Therefore the policy has died a brutal and horrible death :-)  We are now  
likely to take the route suggested in the "Final Report of the Committee on  
the Status of Women Graduate Students and Faculty in the College of  
Engineering" where we post a sign that tells people what to do and who to  
contact if they encounter a situation that makes them uncomfortable.

Thank you everyone (eps, Carl, Seth and Bruce!) for all your discussion on  
the matter.  Your input has proved to be invaluable to us.  We appreaciate  
the rational discussions.

Paul Sears
Engineering Computing Center
University of Houston


