	Indeed. The Comics Code is a story that should be much more
widely known, especially among advocates of freedom of expression. It's
extremely relevant these days, in terms of debates over labeling and
threats of government regulation. In brief, it stands as a monumental
victory of repression and a stern warning to never, ever accept such
"compromises" (labeling), no matter how innocous they might sound.
Politicians leave office, hot issues fade, laws can be struck down, but
self-censorship tends to go on and on.
	I got into a 100K+ thread over the Comics Code in
rec.arts.comics.misc some months ago, so I'll hardly attempt a complete
account. The original version (it's since been loosened, in 1969) is
given below. The only non-fan reference I know is "_Comix; a History of
Comic Books in America_" Les Daniels, 1970, Outerbridge and Dienstfrey;
distributed by Dutton. It has a chapter on this topic. There are a few
errors, but that's still the only outside study of which I'm aware.
	Note that viewing the conflict as "'government' vs. everyone else"
is too simplistic. I believe the forces in dispute are better understood
as publishers who wanted to produce "Archie"-type innocous pap,
publishers who wanted to expand the medium into the great fundamentals
of art (sex and death and politics), and a wide array of censors. To
compress greatly, the publishers who could make a profit under severe
restrictions appeased (and sometimes were) the censors, and used
goverment and private power to gain a market advantage over their rivals.
We may see the same story played out regarding video games or rap music.

