    Yes, in an ideal world all parents would be responsible for their
    children's TV viewing habits. However, given that this is NOT the case,
    what is to be done ?  Should we ignore what may be the harmful effects
    of TV violence on those children whose parents are not as vigilant as
    they could be, in the name of "Freedom of the Press", or some other
    wonderful principle ?
       This is analogous to the argument that some use against sex education
    in schools, i.e. that children SHOULD be learning this at home...But,
    fortunately, we agree (most of us, I think, anyway), that it is in the
    best interests of society that _someone_ teach children about sex. And
    given that in all too many cases it does not get accomplished at home,
    as it would be in a perfect world, then it must get done at school.
       In the same way, I see no loss to society, and much potential gain,
    that such crap as the Power Rangers is removed from the TV schedule.
    The interests of society in producing more healthy children must take
    precedence over what some may see as "censorship".
       By the way, do those who complain about banning stuff like this think
    it is also an assault on liberty that shows with sexual content are
    not shown on YTV ?  Why, as a society, do we take such measures to shield
    our children from TV shows and movies depicting sexual relations (even
    gentle, loving "normal" ones), while at the same time making it acceptable
    to let them go and see Arnold or Sylvester or whoever blowing people away
    by the hundreds ?
       In a society that glorifies violence, while being so puritanical about
    about normal human relationships, it is really quite laughable that some
    people think that they must man the battlements to guard against censorship !
    I really do not believe that censorship ranks very high on the list of things
    that need fixing in our society.
