Something to think about....

The organizer of the University of Kansas student organization, Campus
Internet Association, removed a student's home page from the campus-wide
information system (KUFACTS), because the student's home page contained a
link to a server in France that contained graphic pictures of women. 
While we agree that the student organization should set it's own rules and
enforce them, this incident brought us to the larger question of the
University's liability for copywrite infringment. For example, if a
student's home page contains a link to a document that is copywrited, and
permission was not granted to publish the document, what is the
Universities liability for copywrite infringement? Also, our Internet
provider, MIDNet, states that their lines may not be used to transfer
obsene material.  If a minor telnets into KUFACTS and gets to a link where
he can download pornographic material (possibly copywrited also), is the
University liable when the parents want revenge? 

The only conclusion we can come to is to warn students about copywrite 
laws, pornography laws, ect.,  with the idea that if they are informed, 
then they can decide whether or not to publish or link to those sources.  

We do not know what our liability is in so far as our lines being used to 
carry questionable packets. It is the feeling here that since we are 
not encouraging copywrite violations or porn we can't be responsible for 
the traffic, however the university lawyers are getting nervous.  We have 
a techno-phobic administration and they are also unsure.  The only way we 
can see to keep any of this from happening is to not HAVE an Internet 
connection....Since we can't possibly monitor Web links from KUFACTS.  It 
seems like it must be the responsiblity of the parents, or the person 
publiching copywrited material without permission, not the university.  
However logical this argument, I'm not a lawyer, so I don't know for 
certain. 

I wanted to start a discussion on this topic to see if other universities 
have had similiar discussions and what conclusions they came up with.

Laura

