We're having this discussion right now on a university-wide news group.  One
guy even used the Steve Jackson Games case as an example for why the
university should be careful what kind of access it provides!

Also, there was a comment about the Software Publishers Association (SPA)
"breathing down the university's neck" about copyright infringement. 

I've been holding forth that common carrier status should apply in these
cases, and that in no instance should the university take on the role of
censor, because 1) it's technically unfeasible 2) it violates the spirit of
open communication and discussion a university should represent and defend
and 3) if attempted, it may actually increase the university's liability as
an accessory in copyright infringement (see the FAQ list at
http://www.eff.org/CAF).  I also argue that an analogy to copy machines in
the library also holds--just because the university supplies copy machines,
is it liable if a student uses those copy machines to violate a copyright?


There's much in the CAF archive about censorship of objectionable material,
but the issue of copyright infringement is another area that's not well
represented on the site.   

From caf-talk Caf Nov  4 13:23:48 1994
From: kadie@hal.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Newsgroups: comp.org.eff.talk,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,alt.censorship
Subject: Re: CMU bans alt.sex
Date: 4 Nov 94 18:22:14 GMT
Message-ID: <kadie.783928191@hal.cs.uiuc.edu>

The "Freedom to Read" statement statment of the American Library
Association and dozens of other organizations says:

  There is no place in our society for efforts to coerce the taste of
  others, to confine adults to the reading matter deemed suitable for
  adolescents, or to inhibit the efforts of writers to achieve
  artistic expression.

Some questions about the ban:

1. Who exactly created this policy? How was it approved? Was
   university legal counsel involved? The University Senate?  Were
   computer users and intellectual freedom experts (e.g. librarians)
   involved?

2. Exactly what laws are involved? What exactly do they say? Many such
   laws exempt universities. For example, Iowa State University
   restricted alt.drugs citing an harmful-to-minors law that explictly
   excluded universities. (Yes, "alt.drugs". See question 5).

3. Does university legal counsel think the state law is
   Constitutional?  Do other experts?

4. Does this policy apply to all University forums and media or only
   to computer fourms and media? For example, will the University
   library start restricting access by 17-year-old students?  If only
   computers, why the double standard?

5. Were the banned groups selected with care? For example, "alt.sex"
   is to be banned, but I don't think it fits the stated criteria. As
   The American Library Assocation's statement on "Challenged
   Materials" says:

  "Freedom of expression is protected by the Constitution of the United
  States, but constitutionally protected expression is often separated
  from unprotected expression only by a dim and uncertain line. The
  Constitution requires a procedure designed to focus searchingly on
  challenged expression before it can be suppressed. An adversary
  hearing is a part of this procedure."

6. The policy seems premised on the "bad apple" theory, that is, if a
   newsgroup contains both "good" and "bad" material, and if it is
   impractical to separate them, then all the material must be banned.
   Do university legal counsel and experts agree with this theory.  My
   impression was the First Amendment imposes a "good apple" theory.
   For example, the _Miller_ test for obscenity says that if material
   "taken as a whole" has "serious literary, artistic, political, or
   scientific value" then it is not obscene and is protected by the
   Constitution.

- Carl



