Laura-

I believe your university is being just a bit too paranoid here. Your 
university administration should not have anything to worry about, as I 
believe. I guess I should mention that I am not a lawyer, haven't studied 
law, nor plan to. However, what I give here is what I think to be a valid 
argument. Consider this analogy with respect to photocopying and the 
present copyright law. We have notices stating the copyright law (I'm 
sorry, but I don't know the exact one... but I'm sure the most recent) 
next to EVERY copy machine on campus that is on campus grounds (i.e 
library, dormitories...). Now, if a student were to photocopy several 
pages and include them in say...a paper...WITH references to the original 
source, I don't believe the university can be held responsible. Now, if I 
can connect this analogy, in the student's homepage, I'm sure the line 
that connects to the server in France tells the observer/user who is 
currently accessing the homepage, that this server is one containing 
graphic material from France and that it is not his own. I assume this 
would protect the university and the university from any copyright laws 
(domestic and international). If not, rather than just taking his 
homepage away, ask him to either:
	a) Give the homepage address and write a statment to the affect 
that the material is not his own and it is "copyrighted."
	b) Just tell him to remove the link.

However, (an afterthought) most if not all sites on the internet are 
"open-access." So I really don't think that a problem exists anyway in 
the first place. Also, most articles on the internet, if copyrighted, 
usually do give permission to use the articles AS LONG AS IT IS FULLY 
DOCUMENTED/CITED (and sometimes in it's entirety). By placing an article 
on the internet, one inherently allows EVERYBODY on the internet to look 
and view the document. This said, I believe that the university, it's 
administration, and the young student have nothing to worry about.

As far as the incident with the minor linking to a pornographic site... 
well, there you touched on a very hot topic. The internet is not under 
any controls or supervision - it is not policed, and should not be. If 
the student is a minor, the ones to be held responsible are the 
parents/guardians, not the site he finds the pornographic material or 
other "inappropriate" material. The internet, I believe is "USE AT YOUR 
OWN RISK!"

Well, I've said enough. Does anybody else have something to say on this 
issue?

