I don't buy this.  Going back to the bookstore analogy, this is like saying
"if a bookstore owner finds that one book has libel in it, he must stop
selling all books because they may have libel in them".  

The bookstore owner is protected because she can't monitor the continually
changing content of the books in her store, and can only be held liable for
specific books that have been shown to be libelous.  The argument above
equates bulletin boards with books, and holds that a "tainted" bulletin
board must be dropped.  

I say that the more exact analogy, and the one that should apply is equating
books with postings in a bulletin board.  The only way the sysop can be held
liable is if it's shown that the sysop is aware of the obscene contents of a
specific post--which implies that the sysop has read every post--which, like
the bookstore owner reading every book--is impossible.  

It's not enough that a specific bulletin board may contain obscene postings. 
Given the fluidity of content in most electronic many-to-may communications,
any forum may contain obscenity from time to time.  The Canadian suppression
of newsgroups containing details of the Homolka-Teale trial is a perfect
example--when newsgroups containing suppressed details were shut down,
Homolka-Teale postings bled into other groups.  So, if someone posts
off-topic in cmu.general with something obscene, does that make cmu.general
forever suspect and therefore subject to immediate closure?

