In response to a request by Carl Kadie, here is a rough outline of CMU's
governing bodies:

The University Council is made up of the deans and department heads in the
Univerisity.  It has power to make policy decisions.  Bill Arms (vice
president of Computing Services, and my boss' boss' boss) brought this
issue to the University Council which approved it (reluctantly, so I'm
told).

The Faculty Senate represents the University faculty.  My understanding is
that they have power over academic freedom issues.

The Staff Council represents non-union staff at the University.

The Student Senate represents students and has the power to allocate
the student activities fee income.

The only real influence that Student Senate, Staff Council, and
Faculty Senate have in policy decisions is when they are permitted to
appoint representatives to University Committees.  However, if all
three bodies agree on an issue it is unlikely to be ignored by the
administration.

One university committee that could be particularly useful in this
instance is the Human Relations Committee.  This university committee
has student (I think), faculty, staff, and administrative representatives.
Recommendations by this committee are held in high regard by the
administration, and the current makeup of the committee is relatively
positive.  I would not be surprised if the HRC addresses this issue.

