   political, economic, religious, social, minority, and sexual
   issues." [American Library Association's statement on Diversty
   in Collection Development.]

4.  A decision to ban material on legal grounds should be made in the open.

    "Freedom of expression is protected by the Constitution of the
    United States, but constitutionally protected expression is often
    separated from unprotected expression only by a dim and uncertain
    line. The Constitution requires a procedure designed to focus
    searchingly on challenged expression before it can be suppressed.
    An adversary hearing is a part of this procedure." [ALA statement
    on Challenged Materials.]

5.  Those affected by universtiy policy should participate in making it.

    "The student body should have clearly defined means to participate
    in the formulation and application of institutional policy
    affecting academic and student affairs." [American Assocation of
    University Professor, et al.'s "Joint Statement on Rights and
    Freedoms of Students"]

6.  Adults should not be confined to children's material.

    "There is no place in our society for efforts to coerce the
    taste of others, to confine adults to the reading matter
    deemed suitable for adolescents, or to inhibit the efforts
    of writers to achieve artistic expression._ [The Freedom to Read
    Statement of the ALA and many other groups.]
   
7. Censorship should be challenged.

   "Libraries should challenge censorship in the fulfillment of their
    responsibility to provide information and enlightenment." [ALA's
    Library Bill of Rights.]

