Luckily, it may be too late to censor the net.
Cut one link, and another can form, possibly within minutes, but almost 
always without too much trouble.
Ban a newsgroup from a local server, and one can use a public NNTP server. 
Cut direct access to the net, going to e-mail only (an extreme reaction), 
and people can often get the banned material from e-mail gateways and 
mailing lists.
And if a provider or university or whatever completely goes off the net 
(suicidal, usually), one can go elsewhere. Same if they pull people's 
accounts off the system.
If government enforces unfair laws and sentences people to prison for 
exercising their 1st Ammendment rights, there is anon services, 
cryptography, and steganography (e.g. that GIF file of a nice 1957 
Cadillac could have banned material encoded in the lowest bit where it 
would never be noticed, etc).

The Internet was designed to be fault-tolerant, decentralized, and 
capable to provide for miltary communications during/after a _nuclear war_! 
Its going to be pretty hard to stuff the genie back in the bottle, 
people now know what is possible with computer networking. (Hopefully 
with IPng [successor to the current internet protocol] they won't 
try to change this to something allowing greater government control.)




