From caf-talk Caf Nov  6 16:15:03 1994
From: kip@world.std.com (Bob B Chatelle)
Subject:  National Writers Union letter to CMU (draft)
Message-ID: <CyuyED.1FC@world.std.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Nov 1994 18:08:36 GMT

This is a first draft of a letter I hope to send presently to Dr. Robert 
Meharabian, president of Carnegie Mellon University, protesting their 
planned internet censorship.  I post it for general information.



Dr. Robert Mehrabian, President
Carnegie Mellon University

Dear Dr. Mehrabian:

I'm a National Board member and the Political
Issues Chair of the National Writers Union, which
represents nearly 4,000 freelance writers nation-
wide.  We're proud to be affiliated with the
United Auto Workers as UAW Local 1981.

We were astounded to hear that Carnegie Mellon
University has decided to inhibit access to sev-
eral usenet news groups, including
rec.arts.erotica, the entire alt.sex hierarchy,
and the alt.binaries.pictures.erotica hierarchy.
We are further astounded that this decision was
based on a study that--according to its principle
investigator, Martin Rimm--was not even _read_ by
the CMU administration.  Regardless of the
report's contents, we strongly disagree with CMU's
decision.  We believe that members of your commu-
nity are capable for deciding for themselves what
they wish to read or to view.

According to Mr. Rimm, he had informed a member of
the administration that he had found on the usenet
images that had been declared obscene or were sim-
ilar to images now under indictment in very con-
servative areas, such as Utah and Tennessee.
According to a letter to Sam Minter from Marvin
Sibru, the advisor on the project, these included
images found obscene by a Memphis jury in July in
the case of the Amateur Action bulletin board ser-
vice, located in Milpitas, California.

Before taking rash action, we wish that the CMU
administration had informed itself about this par-
ticular case.  Amateur Action is an adults-only
board that charges a membership fee.  It was
busted by a federal undercover agent who joined in
Tennessee under an assumed name and who did charm-
ing things such as attempt to entrap the system
operators by mailing them unsolicited child
pornography.  What the government hopes to accom-
plish in this case is to subject all of interna-
tional cyberspace to the "community standards" of
the most conservative and sexphobic parts of the
US.  The Amateur Action conviction was a shameful
miscarriage of justice, but not a surprising one.
Fortunately, it is under appeal and we in the
National Writers Union hope that justice will pre-
vail.  I find it incredible that CMU would use
this particular verdict to justify limiting what
members of your community may read.

As an internet user, I'm familiar with the news
groups you plan to ban.  A huge variety of mate-
rial is posted in them, and much of it is quite
valuable--safe-sex information, for example.  Some
of the banned groups, such as alt.sex.motss
(members of the same sex), provide community for
sexual minorities.  I'm concerned whether the
investigators might lack understanding of these
communities.  For example, I confess I found Mr.
Rimm's comment, in his letter to Sam Minter, about
"the sociology of sexual deviance and the
(possible) degradation of women" a bit offensive.
I'm a gay man myself, and I've learned that
researchers often misunderstand sexual-minority
people.

We're not persuaded by the argument that Pennsyl-
vania law requires you to censor your students'
reading material.  If that's what Pennsylvania law
requires, then the law is unconstitutional.  More-
over, if you feel such an urgent need to stop stu-
dents from obtaining sex information, why stop at
banning a few news groups?  Students have other
access to such information.  What about the tele-
phone?  Some may even be calling sex lines!  Will
you tap campus phones to make sure no sex talk is
taking place?

If Pennsylvania law required you to teach that the
Holocaust never occurred, we doubt you would be
equally compliant.  I'm old enough to remember the
Big Red Scare of the 50s and how readily colleges
and university abandoned their commitment to aca-
demic freedom to placate Senator McCarthy and his
fellow witch hunters.  It saddens us that this
pattern is being repeated during the Big Sex Scare
of the 80s and 90s, with the role of Joe McCarthy
being played alternately by Jesse Helms and
Catharine MacKinnon.

According to our constitution, "the National Writ-
ers Union is committed to freedom of expression in
all media, including print, film, and electronic
media of any sort."  We take this commitment very
seriously.  In January of this year, our National
Executive Board unanimously adopted a resolution
that included the following sentence: "We urge our
members to join us in opposing _all_ anti-pornog-
raphy and other pro-censorship legislation at the
Federal, state, and local levels."

As writers, we in the National Writers Union real-
ize that cyberspace offers the promise of the
first universally accessible free market of ideas
in world history.  Such a free intellectual
marketplace could enable democracy truly to flour-
ish, both here in America and throughout the
world.

On the other hand, we are also aware that those
who currently possess an unfair share of wealth
and who wield the accompanying unfair share of
political power are threatened by any free market
of ideas.  We realize too that these same people
wish to bring cyberspace under tight state-corpo-
rate control so that they may further profit.  To
do so, they must convince the public that free
speech in cyberspace is dangerous.  Thus the major
media and law-enforcement officials are cooperat-
ing in a campaign to demonize cyberspace in order
to justify its eventual corporate colonization.
Now, I fear, our universities will join them in
this anti-democratic campaign.

If CMU pursues its ill-considered plan of cyber-
censorship, other colleges and universities will
follow suit, using as justification the CMU study.
This will help assure that the evolving National
Information Infrastructure will be much less free
than the internet.  Rather than a marketplace of
ideas, it will resemble the commercial broadcast
media, where the many are encouraged to buy prod-
ucts they don't need and are spoon-fed carefully
censored "information" by the profit-making few.
CMU may win its place in history as the institu-
tion that midwifed this transition by striking the
critical blow that killed free speech in
cyberspace.

We in the National Writers Union know where we
stand, Dr. Mehrabian, on issues of intellectual
and academic freedom.  When faced with unjust
laws, we work to change them--we don't squander
our precious resources to ensure that they are
zealously enforced.  We hope that you will recon-
sider and rescind this onerous censorship policy
before it is implemented.

We'd would be most interested in hearing
your thoughts on this matter. 

Sincerely,

Robert B. Chatelle, National Board Member
Political Issues Chair
